### SwiftLint Configuration

Pulsar uses SwiftLint to enforce code quality. Follow these guidelines when working with SwiftLint:

#### Running SwiftLint

- Run `swiftlint` to check for violations
- Run `swiftlint --fix` to auto-fix simple issues (trailing newlines, whitespace, etc.)
- Pre-commit hooks automatically run SwiftLint on staged files

#### SwiftLint Disable Comments

When code legitimately exceeds SwiftLint limits, use disable comments appropriately:

**File-Level Disables** (at top of file, after header comment):
```swift
// swiftlint:disable file_length
// swiftlint:disable type_body_length
```

**Type-Level Disables** (before struct/class):
```swift
// swiftlint:disable:next type_body_length
struct MyTests {
    // ...
}
```

**Function-Level Disables** (on function line):
```swift
func complexFunction() { // swiftlint:disable:this cyclomatic_complexity
    // ...
}
```

**Function-Level Disables** (before function, no doc comment between):
```swift
// swiftlint:disable:next cyclomatic_complexity function_body_length
func parseError(_ error: Error) -> AppError {
    // ...
}
```

**Critical Rules:**
- `// swiftlint:disable:next` must be immediately before the declaration it disables
- Do NOT place doc comments between the disable comment and the function
- For function-level disables, use `// swiftlint:disable:this` on the function line, or place `// swiftlint:disable:next` directly before with no doc comment in between
- File-level disables go at the top of the file (after header comment block)

#### Test File Exceptions

Comprehensive test files may legitimately exceed normal limits. When justified, use file-level disables:

```swift
// swiftlint:disable file_length type_body_length
// Justification: Comprehensive test suite requires extensive coverage
@Suite("ActivityService Tests")
struct ActivityServiceTests {
    // 500+ lines of comprehensive tests
}
```

**Acceptable exceptions for test files:**
- File length (500+ lines for comprehensive suites)
- Type body length (300+ lines for test structs)
- Function complexity (helper functions with multiple strategies)
- Always document the justification in a comment

#### Common Violations and Fixes

**Cyclomatic Complexity:**
- Refactor complex functions into smaller helpers
- Use disable comments only when refactoring would reduce readability

**Function Body Length:**
- Extract logic into private helper methods
- Use disable comments for functions that are inherently long (e.g., parsers, formatters)

**File Length:**
- Consider splitting large files into logical modules
- Use disable comments for files that are appropriately organized but long (e.g., comprehensive test suites)

**Orphaned Doc Comment:**
- Place doc comments after disable comments, or use `// swiftlint:disable:this` on the function line
- Ensure disable comments are immediately before the declaration

**Superfluous Disable Command:**
- Only disable rules that are actually violated
- Remove disable comments when violations are fixed

#### Pre-Commit Hook Integration

- Pre-commit hooks automatically run SwiftLint on staged files
- Commits are blocked if violations are found
- Fix violations before attempting to commit
- Use `swiftlint --fix` for auto-fixable issues
- The hook only checks staged files, so unstage unrelated changes if needed

## SwiftUI Best Practices

- Use `@Query` for SwiftData reactive data fetching (default pattern)
- Use `@Observable` for AppState and optional ViewModels
- Use `@State` for local view state
- Use `@Environment` for dependency injection
- Use `@Binding` for two-way data flow
- Use `.task { }` modifier for async work tied to view lifecycle
- Use `NavigationStack` (iOS 16+) instead of `NavigationView`
- Direct service calls from views (default pattern)
- Add ViewModels only when complexity justifies it (3+ data sources, complex state, extensive testing)

### Reusable Content Views

For reusable content views (pure UI presentation components), use simple data types:

```swift
// Good: Content view with simple data types
struct StatCard: View {
    let title: String
    let value: String
    let icon: String
    
    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 8) {
            Image(systemName: icon)
            Text(value)
            Text(title)
        }
    }
}

// Bad: Content view with SwiftData model
struct ActivityCard: View {
    let activity: Activity  // ❌ Avoid SwiftData models in content views
    
    var body: some View {
        // ...
    }
}

// Good: Root view passes simple data to content view
struct ActivityRow: View {
    let activity: Activity
    @Query private var profiles: [Profile]
    
    var body: some View {
        ActivityRowContent(
            name: activity.name,
            distance: activity.distance,
            duration: activity.duration,
            date: activity.startDate
        )
    }
}

// Good: Content view with simple types
struct ActivityRowContent: View {
    let name: String
    let distance: Double
    let duration: Double
    let date: Date
    
    var body: some View {
        // Pure UI presentation
    }
}
```

**Content View Guidelines:**
- Accept simple types: `String`, `Int`, `Double`, `Date`, `URL`, `Bool`, `Color`, `Image`
- Avoid SwiftData models (`Activity`, `Profile`, etc.) - extract data in root view
- No `@Query` or `@Environment(\.modelContext)` in content views
- No Service calls or ViewModels in content views
- Makes views reusable, testable, and preview-friendly

For detailed guidelines on Root/Content view patterns, see [swiftui-view-composition.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/swiftui-view-composition.mdc).

## Performance

- Prefer value types (structs, enums) when appropriate
- Use `lazy var` for expensive computations
- Use `@Query` for reactive data (no manual state duplication)
- Cache expensive computations
- Services are singletons to avoid unnecessary instantiation

